\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{What a neutral loop probes}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{The spatial-loop operators of \ref{fdip-00ND} and their
 vacuum-subtracted Euclidean correlations are probes used in
 the glueball sector of pure SU(3) lattice gauge theory.
 \citet{arXiv v2, Section II, pp. 2-3, equation (1)}{morningstar1999glueball}
 constructs traced-loop combinations in cubic, parity and
 charge-conjugation channels and subtracts the vacuum
 expectation in the scalar even channel. This is a comparison
 of observables and symmetry labels; its improved anisotropic
 lattice action is distinct from the heat-kernel transfer used
 in \ref{fdip-00ND}.}

\p{A cubic scalar is a representation of the finite lattice
 symmetry group, and does not by itself identify continuum
 spin zero. A spectral coefficient in \ref{fdip-00N7}
 measures overlap with a finite-volume eigenspace; it need
 not isolate one particle. These formulas establish neither
 glueball formation nor confinement, a predicted physical
 spectrum, or a volume- and spacing-uniform Yang–Mills mass gap.}

\p{The finite-volume self-adjoint Hamiltonian gives unitary
 real-time evolution on the same complex physical Hilbert
 space by \ref{fdip-00NA}. This does not construct a
 Poincaré representation, relativistic spectrum condition or
 S-matrix for these gauge fields. An arbitrary sandwich
 transfer's logarithm is also not automatically a local
 Kogut–Susskind differential Hamiltonian; identifying one
 requires an additional continuous-time construction with its
 domains and normalization.}

\p{The [finite Abelian charged-matter comparison](fdip-00JB)
 has its own fermionic transfer and continuum QED questions.
 The present neutral pure-gauge operators contain no quark
 fields, so their glueball-sector interpretation supplies
 neither full QCD with quarks nor full hadron physics.}
